# Your own program in a slide

This page gathers, in one checklist, what the examples show one piece at a time: how to take your own C++
OpenGL program (with GLFW) and show it in a slide. Each step links to the example where it is explained in
detail. If your program uses the CGP library, see [the CGP case](#with-cgp) at the end of the page.

## 1. Copy the bridge into your project

From [the zip of the repository](../../code/interactive-3d-slides.zip) (or of
[example 1](../../code/a1-opengl-cube.zip)), copy the three files of `bridge/`:

- `slide_bridge.hpp` and `slide_bridge.cpp`: add them to the sources of your program. They read the settings
  given by the slide, and depend only on the standard library.
- `slide-frame.html`: the page that shows your program in the frame of the slide (step 4).

## 2. Adapt the program for the web

The program compiles for the web with Emscripten (WebGL 2, which corresponds to OpenGL ES 3.0). Four places
differ from the desktop version; in [example 1](../02_cube/index.html#main-cpp) they are marked
with `#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__`:

- **The headers:** `<GLES3/gl3.h>` (and `<emscripten/emscripten.h>`, `<emscripten/html5.h>`) instead of your
  OpenGL loader (glad, glew).
- **The first line of the shaders:** `#version 300 es` and `precision highp float;` instead of
  `#version 330 core`.
- **The size of the window:** the size of the canvas of the page,
  `emscripten_get_canvas_element_size("#canvas", &width, &height)`, used for `glfwCreateWindow` and read again at
  each frame for the viewport. Ask GLFW for an OpenGL ES 3.0 context (`GLFW_CLIENT_API`, `GLFW_OPENGL_ES_API`).
- **The main loop:** move the body of your `while` loop into a function `frame()`, and give it to the browser:

  ```cpp
  #ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
      emscripten_set_main_loop(frame, 0, 1);
  #else
      while (!glfwWindowShouldClose(window))
          frame();
  #endif
  ```

*Rem. Some OpenGL functions do not exist in WebGL, such as `glPolygonMode` (wireframe) or wide lines: see the
notes of [example 4](../05_buttons/index.html) and [example 6](../07_readout/index.html).*

## 3. Declare the settings

At the start of `main()`, read the arguments, then declare the variables that the slide may set:

```cpp
#include "slide_bridge.hpp"

int main(int argc, char* argv[])
{
    slide::parse(argc, argv);                          // the settings of the address: ?speed=0.2 gives --speed 0.2
    slide::param("speed", speed, 0.0f, 1.0f, 0.01f);   // a float, with its range and its step
    slide::param("wireframe", wireframe);              // a bool
    slide::action("reset", [] { reset_scene(); });     // a button
    // ... the rest of your program
}
```

The page builds its panel from these declarations ([example 2](../03_panel/index.html)); the buttons of the slide
use the same names ([example 4](../05_buttons/index.html)). The full list is in the [reference](../../02_more/02_reference/index.html).

## 4. Build for the web

Add the link options of Emscripten to the web build. With CMake:

```cmake
if(EMSCRIPTEN)
    target_link_options(project PRIVATE
        -sUSE_GLFW=3 -sMIN_WEBGL_VERSION=2 -sMAX_WEBGL_VERSION=2
        -sEXPORTED_FUNCTIONS=_main,_slide_set,_slide_describe -sEXPORTED_RUNTIME_METHODS=ccall)
endif()
```

- `-sEXPORTED_FUNCTIONS` and `-sEXPORTED_RUNTIME_METHODS` let the page talk to the bridge.
- If the program reads files (shaders, meshes, textures), add `--preload-file <folder>`: they are packed into
  `project.data`, next to `project.js`.
- *Rem. The module [raw_opengl_demo.cmake](../../code/examples/cmake/raw_opengl_demo.cmake) of the examples does
  all this, and can be included as in [example 1](../02_cube/index.html#cmakelists-txt).*

Then build, with the Emscripten SDK activated:

```
emcmake cmake -S . -B build_web
cmake --build build_web
```

`build_web/` then contains `project.js` and `project.wasm` (and `project.data` with `--preload-file`). Copy
`slide-frame.html` next to them, as `index.html`: this is the page of the demo. If your executable is not named
`project`, change `script: 'project.js'` in the block `CONFIG` at the top of `slide-frame.html`.

## 5. Put the demo in a slide

Copy the folder of the demo (`index.html`, `project.js`, `project.wasm`...) next to your slides, for instance in
`demo/`, then:

- **reveal.js:** a frame in a `<section>`, `<iframe class="demo" data-src="demo/index.html?speed=0.2"></iframe>`
  (see [slides.html of example 1](../02_cube/index.html#slides-html)).
- **lhtml-site:** the folder in `src/_demo/`, and in the slide `demo::../_demo/index.html?speed=0.2`
  (see [the lhtml-site slide of example 1](../02_cube/index.html#lhtml-site)).

Show the slides through a web server (`python3 -m http.server 8080` in their folder), not as files.

- **&gt;** Check that the demo fills its frame, that the settings of the address are applied, and that the arrow
  keys still change the slide after a click in the demo.

## With CGP

A CGP project already builds for the web. Three steps are enough ([example 3](../04_cgp/index.html)):

- copy `slide_bridge.hpp` and `slide_bridge.cpp` into `src/`;
- add the link options of the bridge to the web build of `CMakeLists.txt`:
  `-sEXPORTED_FUNCTIONS=_main,_slide_set,_slide_describe -sEXPORTED_RUNTIME_METHODS=ccall`;
- use `slide-frame.html` as the page of the demo (copied as `index.html` next to `project.js`).

Then declare the settings as in step 3, for instance in `scene.cpp` ([example 4](../05_buttons/index.html)).

